{
  "dom_id": "question-131248",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "$f(6)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n- an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and\n\n- a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
